  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper trains a domain-specific SAE on a pretrained SAE’s reconstruction residuals and reports how its own adaptation method performs. It does not test the claim’s linear-predictability, cross-size error scaling, matched-random-error, error-type, or downstream-analysis comparisons.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports results for SAE Boost trained on reconstruction residuals, including domain-specific EV and LLM cross-entropy improvements and general-domain effects. These results are recorded as findings about the residual-based method, not as tests of the listed claim versions. Baseline-only results used for comparison and alternative adaptation-method comparisons are not separate rows.",
    "test_reason": "The paper trains a residual SAE on a pretrained SAE's reconstruction error and reports only its own method's EV and LLM CE gains on domain data. It shows that domain-specific error holds learnable sparse features. It never measures linear predictability of the error, error scaling across SAE sizes, matched random-error comparisons, a linear vs nonlinear decomposition, or circuit and steering analyses with and without the error term. Its results therefore bear on no version of the claim.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct as paraphrased. The authors add that residual features are novel (lower cosine similarity to base features) and interpretable (higher detection and fuzzing scores), and that multiple domain boosts can be combined.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "This paper trains an additional SAE on domain-specific reconstruction residuals for adaptation; it does not reproduce the original study’s measurements of dark-matter structure or scaling.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Outcomes vary by domain and backbone SAE. Residual SAE training duration matters: the paper reports general-domain degradation when trained on fewer than 100M tokens and less than 1% explained-variance change beyond 200M tokens. Residual top-k also changes domain EV and sparsity (Table 12).",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "A 1024-latent residual SAE trained on a pretrained SAE's domain-specific reconstruction error raises domain EV by 8.5–59% and lowers LLM CE by 4.7–56%. It changes general-domain metrics by under 1% once trained past roughly 200M tokens.",
        "proposed_claim": "Part of a general SAE's error on out-of-distribution domains consists of sparse, interpretable domain features that a residual SAE can learn without degrading general performance.",
