    "peer_reviewed": "COLM 2025 conference paper",
    "problems": "The reported results do not test the claim versions’ specified comparisons. In particular, there is no linear-predictability analysis, SAE-size sweep, matched-size random-error comparison, decomposition of error types, or circuit/steering analysis with and without the error term. The main table results give no uncertainty estimates.",
    "rows_left_out": "Tables 3–9 compare SAE Boost with other domain-adaptation methods and multi-domain configurations; excluded because they primarily compare the paper’s method with alternative methods rather than measuring a claim version. Table 12 reports sensitivity to residual top-k, and feature-interpretability results in Appendix Tables 10–11 and Figures 2, 5–6 do not measure the listed claim versions.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No regression of SAE error on input activations and no linearly predictable share reported."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "No SAE size sweep, no per-token error norm prediction, and no overlap analysis of failing contexts."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "LLM CE is reported for reconstructions, but there is no comparison with random errors of equal norm."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "The residual SAE learns features from the error, but the error is never split into linear and nonlinear parts. The paper does not test whether some part resists dictionary learning, though residual error e2 remains."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "No circuit or steering analysis with versus without the error term."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "No comparison of SAE training choices or architectures for their effect on the size of the dark matter. The residual approach is domain adaptation, not a test of training artifacts."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "The paper cites Muhamed et al. ('Decoding dark matter') but not the original dark-matter paper. Its framing that error contains missed rare or domain features loosely echoes V4's linear part, but no decomposition is done, so no verdict is given. Table 9 lists the Russian general baseline as 0.720, while Table 2 gives 0.719. The main tables give no uncertainty estimates."
  },
  "versions": [
